<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matsudaira Harusato</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1751">1751</a>–<a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Matsudaira Fumai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matsudaira Harusato was the seventh lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsue">Matsue</a> domain in Izumo, better known by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> name Fumai. With his ministers he restored the domain's finances through land reform, flood control and ginseng production. He became one of the great tea masters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>, founding a school of tea and assembling a famous collection of tea utensils.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matsudaira-harusato.jpg" alt="Matsudaira Harusato" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matsudaira_Fumai.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
